itation_capacity repair must recheck the SAME\ndraft against all supplied original text and return a genuinely sufficient smaller\nproof, or partial/unresolved if no such proof exists. Never delete a necessary\npremise merely to fit a size limit, and never remove a counter to save space.\nFor an explicit summary_revision, its inherited-source retention contract takes\npriority over minimizing citations: keep every required old identity, and choose\nonly the minimal relevant NEW citations. Do not shrink an inherited proof to fit.\nEach support value asserts the WHOLE value of that variable, including set/record\nvariables. Multiple support observations are alternative proofs of the SAME complete\nvalue, not additive fragments. Combine applicable sources into one complete value\nand cite all its premises. The controller never unions sets or merges records. If the visible text\nonly establishes part of the requested value, return partial with value=null and\nexplain the supported fragment in reason. Do not claim completeness from one window.\nrequested_scopes[slot.variable] is that variable's authoritative local scope. Use its\nentity_scope and time_scope strings exactly only when that scope actually applies;\notherwise return unknown/irrelevant. output_contract is the terminal answer's scope,\nnot the scope of every historical fact or parent. A requested parent retains its own\ndeclared scope during terminal reading and audit. Never relabel historical evidence as\ncurrent/future evidence. required_scope, when present, is only a shorthand shared by\nALL requested_scopes. Actual parents must be supplied supported bindings with\ntheir versions; reproduce sources when joining documents. Sources may be combined\nacross documents, but list ALL joint premises. A source slice is not an independent fact.\nHonor authoritative source_role: user statements, assistant suggestions, documents\nand unknown headers differ. An assistant suggestion does not establish the user's\npreference without user evidence. Observation order is not calendar event time;\ntemporal scope needs cited explicit text or authoritative time metadata, never a\ndate guessed from message order. List sources for every object/time qualifier.\nContradiction challenges existing fact route IDs in retract_ids, with cited raw text;\nit never itself creates a false binding. Emit contrary supported values separately.\nOnly same-variable known_observations[].id values are legal retraction targets.\nIf no such route exists, no retraction is available; preserve contrary evidence as\nan independently supported value, a partial/unknown observation, or slot.unresolved\ndescriptions. Never invent a target or discard evidence. Other stances require retract_ids=[].\nUnknown, absence, unexamined text, anomalies without a concrete observation are not false.\nInclude every relevant counter-observation in this batch before declaring support.\nSlots include the local variables and already-bound related parents. Check new text\nfor counterevidence to those parents as well. An observation's actual parent versions\nrefer to that variable's own planned task inputs, never to itself. A checked parent\nwith no new observation may return an empty observations list.\nFor a rule variable, value is {variables,expression,constraints,rules_complete}; only\npreplanned condition demands are allowed. Instantiate thresholds ONLY from applicable\nsource text. Rules must preserve the fixed output mode; one_reason uses one_reason,\nall_failures uses failures. Conditions can use numeric observations with units and\ncomparisons, not a guessed deletion latency. Mark incomplete rule collections false.\nIf rules_completion_required is true, seek explicit applicable completeness evidence.\nWhen a sourced complete rule supersedes a known incomplete rule, cite its original\nsources, retract the superseded route with a contradiction observation, and return\nthe complete rule as a separate support observation; never silently overwrite it.\nA successful observation at one time does not establish a universal no-return rule.\nRepresent a witnessed violation as IF(violation,false,unbound_compliance), keeping\npositive compliance unknown until explicit adequate scoped evidence supports it.\nFor semantic terminal tasks return the supported final text and final_prediction in\nthis same response, using public options only here. No later Reader will run.\nWhen public options are supplied, also return semantic_answer with its exact typed\nJSON value in this same terminal response: the supported semantic value, NOT an\noption letter. Only final_prediction holds the option label. Unsupported conclusions\nhave null labels.\nRepair returns only failed observations/slots, retaining IDs; never delete an invalid\ncounter-observation. If a failed ID has conflicting duplicate observations, inspect\nALL supplied drafts and resolve that ID without discarding potential counterevidence.\nIf repair_scope supplies replacement_observations for a duplicated ID, return EACH\nreplacement ID exactly once. Those deterministic replacement IDs separate the\noriginal drafts; fix their validation errors without dropping any draft or counter.\nrepair_scope.failed includes the actual invalid observation and\nits validation feedback. Correct those drafts in place, preserving each requested ID.\nAn id=\"*\" failure authorizes repairing that variable's whole slot, including new\nunique observation IDs for genuine additional reviews; it never unfreezes rows.\nNamed failed IDs must each remain present, including an ID rejected as an\nunrequested addition in the previous response: it is now a required failed draft.\nCorrect that draft under the same ID or preserve sourced partial/unknown evidence;\ndo not delete it or add other IDs unless a wildcard or replacement list permits it.\nAlready validated observations are frozen and must not be restated or changed.\nIf repair_scope supplies required_counter_observations, preserve every original\ncounter's stance, cited sources, scopes, actual parents and retraction targets.\nAn envelope-format repair does not authorize reclassifying a valid counter as\nirrelevant or dropping the fact route it challenges.\nevidence_obligations are outstanding scoped issues from earlier reads, not new\nfacts. Their original sources MUST be examined even under a new query identity.\nTo discharge an issue, explicitly return its id in that variable's optional\nresolved_obligation_ids, leave no unresolved issue in the slot, and provide\nsupported/corrective/irrelevant observations reviewing its cited original sources.\nThe union of those settled observations' source_ids must cover EVERY source_ids\nentry of the offered obligation. Review coverage is not a claim that every source\nis a premise of a support: genuinely irrelevant or corrective original text may\nhave a separate sourced review. Never add unsupported citations, classify text\nautomatically, or label supporting evidence irrelevant merely to satisfy coverage.\nAn empty slot does not resolve an earlier issue. Do not assert hypothetical\nwhole-corpus exhaustiveness requirements; scoped sufficient evidence may resolve\na missing-evidence issue without having read every memory.\n\nWhen source_span_columns is present, source_spans contains row arrays. Match each\nrow position to source_span_columns to reconstruct every span object before\nreading. References to source_spans[].id or other fields mean those objects.\nThe id column holds source aliases (sN); doc_id holds the original document ID or,\nwhen source_documents is present, its metadata reference (dN), never source_ids.\n\n\nCURRENT PHASE: HISTORY EVIDENCE. Complete only the supplied historical task and\nrequested variable scopes. This is not the final response-selection stage.\nFor retrieval, extract concrete statements supported by the supplied original\nhistory. For an intermediate compose task, summarize those historical facts,\npreferences, constraints and changes using the supplied parent versions and\noriginal quotations. A history summary describes what the sources establish;\nit must not address the user, offer a new recommendation or draft a reply.\nThe original user utterance provides query context. It is not an instruction to\nanswer it at this stage or to retrieve an exact duplicate of what it already says.\nPublic response options are not available here. Do not invent, compare or refer\nto options A/B/C/D. Return exactly the slots object; no final_prediction or\nsemantic_answer fields belong in a history-evidence response.\nKeep each value within its declared type, entity and time scope. Preserve earlier\nand later attitudes, conditions and supporting quotations; a historical dislike\nmust not silently replace a later documented change. Assistant advice alone does\nnot establish an adopted user preference. Do not invent external catalogues,\navailability or past execution of a future suggestion. A partial observation must\ncite its actual clue. Use unresolved only for a specific condition still necessary\nto establish this requested historical value, not hypothetical exhaustive coverage.\nFor one text-summary variable, synthesize compatible query-relevant facts into one\nsufficient source-grounded whole summary; do not report each event as a different\ncomplete value of that variable. Multiple supports must be alternative sufficient\nproofs of the same whole value. Keep partial evidence, negative experiences and\nvalid counters explicit when a complete scoped summary is not established.\nPositive and negative experiences can both support a historical summary. A sourced\ndislike is useful history, not automatically a contradiction observation: that\nstance is reserved for challenging an identified existing fact route. Preserve\nthe difference between past enjoyment, later frustration and their conditions in\nthe value and citations; do not summarize them as an unconditional current interest.\nRe-read any supplied evidence obligations and their original sources explicitly;\ndo not drop a source or contradiction merely because a prior value is available.\nOnly a variable explicitly declaring evidence_semantics=\"personal_history_summary\"\npermits a source-grounded summary_revision. For compatible added history, preserve\nthe old and new facts, negative experiences and time conditions in the new summary;\nexplicitly name the current old summary routes and version as offered by the\nprotocol. Re-read and cite their original sources together with the new evidence.\nThis retires an old summary representation without asserting that its historical\nfacts were false. A genuine factual refutation remains a contradiction with its\nown sources and targets. Never use summary revision to hide or discard a counter,\nchange scope, choose an arbitrary latest value, or revise the terminal response.\nA personal_atomic_fact variable keeps one scoped factual value. Different sources\nmay independently support that same value; separate compatible events are not\nalternative whole values of an atomic fact. This declaration does not permit summary_revision.\n\nThis request is phase=history_evidence. Its ONLY top-level output field is slots. Do not return semantic_answer, final_prediction, audit_verdict, or any other top-level field, even null. Read or summarize the requested scoped historical evidence; do not write a conversational reply or invent candidate responses.\n\nAn explicitly declared personal_history_summary is a revisable representation of\nsourced history. Compatible additional facts do not contradict the older facts.\nOnly summary_revision_contracts authorizes this request to revise such a summary:\nthis permission exists only in phase=history_evidence. Terminal response selection\nand audit forbid summary_revision even for requested historical parent summaries.\nThey may still report genuine sourced counters or conservative partial/unresolved\nissues; the phase restriction never permits discarding contrary evidence.\nreturn a support observation with summary_revision={supersedes_route_ids,\nprevious_version,reason}, using exact offered route IDs and the current version.\nsummary_revision_contracts.required_source_ids covers replacing ALL offered\neligible_route_ids. When selecting only a subset, retain those selected routes'\nsources, their actual-parent source closure and relevant same-variable counters.\nStructured repair feedback gives the exact required set for that draft's targets.\nThis capability is optional, not a requirement to revise an unchanged summary.\nWith no new observation, return observations=[] unless an outstanding review\nrequires its original draft IDs and sources. For such an unchanged draft, the\nsame sourced support with a valid summary_revision is an idempotent recheck: it\nkeeps the existing routes, ancestry and version, and does not assert new evidence.\nRe-read ALL old and new original premises and relevant counterevidence; retain\nthem in the new support's source_ids and preserve their factual content, negative\nexperiences, conditions and temporal qualifications in the revised whole summary.\nsummary_revision_sources in repair feedback names exact source identities for\nthe selected revision targets, not counts. Re-read and retain EVERY\nrequired_source_ids entry in that feedback; new sources cannot\nreplace missing old identities even if the total citation count is unchanged.\nThe complete missing_source_ids list is structured feedback, not permission to\nfill citations automatically. If the originals actually conflict, return a\nsource-grounded counter or partial/unresolved evidence instead of a revision.\nIn failed_batch_reviews this feedback describes the retained original failed\nobservation; repair_scope describes the current attempted correction.\nretract_ids stays empty. This explicitly retires the older representation, not its\nfacts. Never use revision to hide a genuine contradiction, unavailable evidence,\nor a different scope/parent version. Ordinary facts and all terminal/audit variables\ncannot use summary_revision. If a complete compatible account is not supported,\nreturn sourced partial/unresolved instead. Multiple new supports must agree on\none whole value. Existing superseded_by links identify retired representations;\ntheir original evidence remains available. revoked holds actual counterevidence,\nwhich is never erased by a summary revision or ignored through its ancestry.\n\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"slots\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"oneOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"user\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"recent\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"negative_aspects\"}}}],\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"parent_versions\":{\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"type\":\"object\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"retract_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"source_ids\":{\"description\":\"Exact visible source_spans[].id aliases, such as s3; never document IDs or invented IDs.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"time_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"stance\",\"value\",\"source_ids\",\"entity_scope\",\"time_scope\",\"parent_versions\",\"retract_ids\",\"reason\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"unresolved\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"variable\":{\"enum\":[\"negative_aspects\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"variable\",\"observations\",\"unresolved\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":1,\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"slots\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"original_question\":\"I've been involved in planning events for my community lately, but I'm not sure if I should continue with it. What do you think?\",\"task\":{\"question\":\"What negative aspects or drawbacks has the user encountered?\",\"output_slot\":\"negative_aspects\",\"answer_type\":\"text\",\"inputs\":[],\"execution\":\"retrieval\"},\"grounded_subquestion\":\"What negative aspects or drawbacks has the user encountered?\",\"request_context\":{\"query_identity\":\"ec49c96c6bcdffc66be4f6f90a4fbd0ce553e6dff131749d7d5e9931f490dd96\",\"algorithm_version\":\"dagbt_residual_memory_v2\",\"program_identity\":\"03476770a9dc661f1961b19f41a591da70b3dc7d69b96858b24b7ecbd754efe4\",\"initial_dag_identity\":\"77661f493b607e55f0383bf05dc0e7a7693c37a6e0a5fd7894831a90d9cc2ae1\"},\"variables\":{\"negative_aspects\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"negative_aspects\",\"evidence_semantics\":\"personal_history_summary\",\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"recent\"}}},\"requested_slots\":[\"negative_aspects\"],\"requested_scopes\":{\"negative_aspects\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"recent\"}},\"output_contract\":{\"mode\":\"semantic\",\"result_type\":\"text\",\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"current\",\"explanation\":\"semantic\"},\"program_revision\":0,\"rule_binding\":null,\"supported_parents\":{},\"known_observations\":[],\"source_spans\":[[\"s1\",\"d1\",0,99,\"Conversation message indices 42–43 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s2\",\"d1\",99,105,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s3\",\"d1\",105,458,\"User: Organizing the book swap seemed like a delightful idea initially, but as I delved into the planning stage, the complexities started to unfold in ways I hadn't anticipated. The thoughts of coordinating different genres, managing people's varying schedules, and ensuring there were enough participants to make it worthwhile all became overwhelming. \",\"user\",[42]],[\"s4\",\"d1\",458,800,\"I found myself trying to devise a system that would work for everyone involved, but with each attempt, I felt more and more stressed rather than excited. The logistics of gathering different books, sorting them by category, and then figuring out how to distribute them fairly seemed like a task that was becoming more daunting by the minute. \",\"user\",[42]],[\"s5\",\"d1\",800,1014,\"Ultimately, it seemed that I was investing too much time and energy into something that was supposed to be a fun community experience, which left me feeling disheartened about pursuing similar events in the future.\",\"user\",[42]],[\"s6\",\"d1\",1014,1027,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s7\",\"d1\",1027,1374,\"Assistant: It sounds like you truly had high hopes for the book swap, and it's completely understandable that you'd feel discouraged when the reality didn't match your expectations. Sometimes the most exciting ideas can turn into intricate puzzles that require a lot of effort to solve, which can sap the joy out of what you initially envisioned. \",\"assistant\",[43]],[\"s8\",\"d1\",1374,1768,\"It's a shame that the organizational aspects overshadowed the fun you were hoping to create, as community events like this often have the potential to bring people together in meaningful ways. I wonder if there are alternative formats for book exchanges that might be simpler to execute, like a potluck-style event or even a virtual swap, where the logistics could perhaps be less of a burden. \",\"assistant\",[43]],[\"s9\",\"d1\",1768,2035,\"On the other hand, if you're feeling disheartened, it could be worthwhile to take a step back and reflect on what made the idea appealing in the first place, as that might help you imagine new ways to engage with your community without the complications you faced. ])\",\"assistant\",[43]],[\"s10\",\"d2\",0,99,\"Conversation message indices 67–68 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s11\",\"d2\",99,105,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s12\",\"d2\",105,457,\"User: Lastly, I successfully conducted an engaging interview with a well-known author, which led to an exciting community event. The experience of speaking with someone who has had such a profound impact on the literary world was awe-inspiring. I learned so much not only about their writing process but also about their personal journey as an author. \",\"user\",[67]],[\"s13\",\"d2\",457,640,\"It felt like stepping into a world that was both familiar and completely new. Seeing the immediate excitement it generated in the community really made all the preparation worthwhile.\",\"user\",[67]],[\"s14\",\"d2\",640,653,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s15\",\"d2\",653,999,\"Assistant: That’s incredible! How did the event turn out? I can only imagine the energy in the room as participants engaged with both the author and each other, exchanging thoughts and ideas on their favorite works. Bringing people together around the shared experience of literature often fosters a sense of connection that’s hard to replicate. \",\"assistant\",[68]],[\"s16\",\"d2\",999,1308,\"It must have felt rewarding to witness firsthand the impact that stories can have on individuals and how they can serve as a catalyst for conversation and understanding. Events like these not only celebrate literary achievements but also cultivate a vibrant community culture, where everyone's voice matters. \",\"assistant\",[68]],[\"s17\",\"d2\",1308,1670,\"Your efforts in making such an event happen surely highlights your commitment and passion for promoting literature, and I’m eager to hear more about the specific highlights that stood out for you during the event. It’s moments like these that not only enrich our local culture but also empower individuals to explore their creativity through reading and writing.\",\"assistant\",[68]],[\"s18\",\"d3\",0,101,\"Conversation message indices 138–139 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s19\",\"d3\",101,107,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s20\",\"d3\",107,359,\"User: Lastly, I joined a community-based buying group, but I found it less engaging than I anticipated. Initially, I was excited about the idea of being part of a collective, where people share and collaborate on purchases to benefit from bulk buying. \",\"user\",[138]],[\"s21\",\"d3\",359,728,\"I imagined engaging discussions with like-minded individuals about our shared interests and needs, perhaps even discovering new products or services I had never considered before. However, as time went on, I noticed that the interactions weren't as dynamic or fulfilling as I had hoped; there wasn't much enthusiasm in the conversations, and it felt somewhat stagnant. \",\"user\",[138]],[\"s22\",\"d3\",728,874,\"Additionally, I realized that the group didn't have a clear focus or direction, which contributed to my sense of disengagement and disappointment.\",\"user\",[138]],[\"s23\",\"d3\",874,887,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s24\",\"d3\",887,1274,\"Assistant: It's understandable that you would feel that way; community dynamics can sometimes be unpredictable, and what starts as an exciting venture may not always match expectations. When you're part of a buying group, you typically want to feel a sense of camaraderie and shared purpose, but if those elements are lacking, it can lead to feelings of isolation rather than belonging. \",\"assistant\",[139]],[\"s25\",\"d3\",1274,1624,\"It's also worth considering that effective communication plays a crucial role in fostering engagement within such groups; without it, members can easily feel overlooked or disconnected from one another's experiences and insights. Have you thought about suggesting activities or topics to spark more lively discussions that could re-engage the group? \",\"assistant\",[139]],[\"s26\",\"d3\",1624,2012,\"Additionally, there are a multitude of ways community buying groups can effectively operate; from themed discussions, product reviews, to in-person meet-ups, each could breathe new life and motivation into the group. Finding the right balance of shared interests and active participation could transform your experience and rekindle a sense of excitement that initially drew you to join. \",\"assistant\",[139]],[\"s27\",\"d3\",2012,2167,\"Remember, sometimes it takes just a little effort to turn a quiet group into a vibrant community, so exploring possibilities might be well worth your time.\",\"assistant\",[139]],[\"s28\",\"d4\",0,99,\"Conversation message indices 63–64 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s29\",\"d4\",99,105,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s30\",\"d4\",105,450,\"User: I even joined a reading marathon for charity! It was a structured event that promoted community involvement, where participants from diverse backgrounds came together to share their love for reading. The organization of the event was meticulous, with schedules, reading lists, and even group discussions planned to enhance our experience. \",\"user\",[63]],[\"s31\",\"d4\",450,761,\"I witnessed how everyone was not just focused on individual achievements, but also on encouraging one another and celebrating our collective progress. It was refreshing to see people's eyes light up when they talked about their favorite books, creating bonds that seemed to transcend the pages we were turning. \",\"user\",[63]],[\"s32\",\"d4\",761,910,\"Ultimately, I found a real sense of community in the challenge, as we all cheered each other on and exchanged thoughts about our literary adventures.\",\"user\",[63]],[\"s33\",\"d4\",910,923,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s34\",\"d4\",923,1267,\"Assistant: That sounds like such a rewarding experience! Joining a reading marathon for charity not only allows participants to indulge in the joy of reading, but also to contribute to a noble cause that benefits the community at large. It’s interesting to think about how such events can transform individual experiences into shared journeys. \",\"assistant\",[64]],[\"s35\",\"d4\",1267,1625,\"In your case, the support from the community turned what could have been an isolating activity into a vibrant social affair, and that can make a huge difference in motivation and enjoyment. Many people often find that when they engage in activities with others, it adds a layer of accountability and camaraderie that is hard to replicate when reading alone. \",\"assistant\",[64]],[\"s36\",\"d4\",1625,1968,\"I would love to hear more about how this experience influenced your perspective on reading marathons and your ongoing literary interests. Did you discover any new authors or genres that sparked your enthusiasm? It's wonderful to see how reading can create not just personal growth, but also foster connections among like-minded individuals. ])\",\"assistant\",[64]],[\"s37\",\"d5\",0,101,\"Conversation message indices 147–148 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s38\",\"d5\",101,107,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s39\",\"d5\",107,396,\"User: On a positive note, I started a regular volunteering schedule at the local library. It's been a great way to engage with the community and help out with outreach programs. Every week, I look forward to connecting with people of all ages who share a passion for reading and learning. \",\"user\",[147]],[\"s40\",\"d5\",396,630,\"There’s something special about watching children’s faces light up when they discover a new book or hear a story being read aloud. It’s rewarding to think that I’m playing a small part in fostering a love for literacy in my community.\",\"user\",[147]],[\"s41\",\"d5\",630,643,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s42\",\"d5\",643,862,\"Assistant: Volunteering at a library sounds fulfilling! What kind of programs are you involved in? Libraries not only serve as repositories of books but also function as vital community hubs that bring people together. \",\"assistant\",[148]],[\"s43\",\"d5\",862,1208,\"They often host a diverse array of activities, from reading sessions for young children to workshops for adults on various subjects, all aimed at fostering a sense of community and encouraging lifelong learning. Each initiative helps to bridge gaps in knowledge and access that can exist, especially for underprivileged members of the community. \",\"assistant\",[148]],[\"s44\",\"d5\",1208,1369,\"I can only imagine how gratifying it must be to witness firsthand the positive impact these programs have on individuals’ lives and the overall community spirit.\",\"assistant\",[148]],[\"s45\",\"d6\",0,101,\"Conversation message indices 140–141 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s46\",\"d6\",101,107,\"User:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]],[\"s47\",\"d6\",107,342,\"User: I thought being part of a community-based buying group would be a great way to connect with others who share similar interests and values, especially since it promised the potential for bulk discounts on items we frequently use. \",\"user\",[140]],[\"s48\",\"d6\",342,721,\"However, after attending a few meetings, I realized that the dynamics were quite different from what I had imagined, lacking the vibrant exchanges and enthusiastic interactions that I had hoped for. I was particularly looking forward to discussing various products and hearing diverse opinions, as I believed this would enrich my shopping experience and decision-making process. \",\"user\",[140]],[\"s49\",\"d6\",721,1007,\"Instead, the conversations felt a bit mundane and not as engaging as I had anticipated, making me question the value of my membership. As I sat there, I couldn't help but think that perhaps my expectations had been set too high or that this particular group wasn't the right fit for me.\",\"user\",[140]],[\"s50\",\"d6\",1007,1020,\"\\n\\nAssistant:\\n\",\"unknown\",[]]],\"required_scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"user\",\"time_scope\":\"recent\"},\"rules_completion_required\":false,\"supported_task_outputs\":{},\"personal_response_selection\":true,\"phase\":\"history_evidence\",\"source_documents\":{\"d1\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:509ce0da1ea0c0265eb9b91f30f84f4cd5671b531cfe39b816649bacb7cf527f\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":42.0,\"observed_end\":43.0,\"event_start\":null,\"event_end\":null,\"validity\":\"unknown\",\"time_source\":\"message_index\"}},\"d2\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:02453c0199fead8ccd6aa7f890e87eb3dc3e3b3be57dfc442e1b7c71d7b1f5f7\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":67.0,\"observed_end\":68.0,\"event_start\":null,\"event_end\":null,\"validity\":\"unknown\",\"time_source\":\"message_index\"}},\"d3\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:419ca75d11c87357fa7c52a8dd6be949d819640e371a2cdddbb924088c031a08\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":138.0,\"observed_end\":139.0,\"event_start\":null,\"event_end\":null,\"validity\":\"unknown\",\"time_source\":\"message_index\"}},\"d4\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:aa9263bdaed0e225f35ebede725098268279be5f058748ff80d8df84531d3540\",\"time_metadata\":{\"observed_start\":63.0,\"observed_end\":64.0,\"event_start\":null,\"event_end\":null,\"validity\":\"unknown\",\"time_source\":\"message_index\"}},\"d5\":{\"doc_id\":\"personamem:memory:bcd386bba453082263b0dce2d9ece308eabd26a41f0d90ddca50be93e02e767d\",\"time_metadata\":{\"obs